(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :finalize-masonry-work-decision :authorize-wall-construction :proceed-with-masonry-work :override-safety-officer-judgment :override-foreman-judgment)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2799007111-cloud-itonami-isco-7111-house-builders-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "proceed with the masonry work" "proceed with the wall-laying work" "finalize the masonry work decision" "authorize the wall construction" "finalize the mortar-joint decision" "override the foreman's safety judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override the site safety officer's judgment" "override foreman safety judgment" "override site safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799007112), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}"), (:actor/domain :construction/masonry-site-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :worker-verified :site-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "7112"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007112: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7112 Bricklayers and Related Workers job-site scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7112\n-- Bricklayers and Related Workers** only, Wave3 (production/trades)\nof the rollout. This is a retry of this exact task: a prior attempt\nwas cut off early with no repository created, so this session started\nclean (fresh `gh api` 404 confirmed before scaffolding).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7112\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7112\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Bricklayers and Related\nWorkers\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no\nrepository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7112` (`gh\napi` 404 confirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion\nregistry edit promote the `7112` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nBricklayers perform structural/masonry construction work on active job\nsites -- real physical-safety stakes (falling materials, wall\ncollapse, scaffold hazards). The controlling constraint for this\nvertical is that the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op\nthat directly finalizes a masonry-work-execution decision (e.g.\ndeciding to proceed with a specific wall-laying or mortar-joint step),\nor overrides a site safety officer's or foreman's judgment -- those\ndecisions must always be a hard permanent block or an always-escalate\nop, never auto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to\nJOB-SITE SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs\nmasonry work itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set) -- cloned\nand read in full in this session. The physical-safety-domain shape\n(closed allowlist, always-escalate safety flag, content-based\nscope-exclusion with a self-trip regression test) was mirrored closely\nfrom the most directly comparable sibling already landed in this\nexact wave -- job-site scheduling/logistics coordination for a\ndifferent construction trade with the identical closed op-allowlist\nshape --\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111)\n(House Builders) -- also cloned and read in full, then adapted from\nbuilder/house-building/framing-and-foundation vocabulary to\nworker/masonry/wall-laying-and-mortar-joint vocabulary for this\noccupation.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7112` as an OSS job-site\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for bricklayers, following the\nSAME governed-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`\nactor in this fleet: **Bricklaying Advisor ⊣ BricklayingGovernor** on a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a\ndifferent library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **job-site scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct masonry-work-execution authority** -- it must\nNEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a masonry-work-execution decision (e.g. a specific\nwall-laying or mortar-joint step), or any override of a site safety\nofficer's or foreman's judgment. These are not merely gated behind\nescalation -- they are structurally absent from the op-allowlist\nentirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a structural-integrity/scaffold-\n  hazard/crew-fatigue concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- masonry-materials procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nmasonry-work-execution decision or a site-safety-officer/foreman-\njudgment override. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:finalize-masonry-work-decision` cannot even be proposed by\nthe reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions only\never emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,\nindependent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names five\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`bricklaying.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-masonry-work-decision`, `:authorize-wall-construction`,\n`:proceed-with-masonry-work`, `:override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-foreman-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is verified by\ndedicated unit tests\n(`bricklaying.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-masonry-work-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-wall-construction`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-foreman-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Worker unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered worker record (`:no-worker`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the worker is.\n2. **Site unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently\n   registered job site (`:no-site`). Together with check 1, this\n   directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a worker/site\n   record must be independently verified/registered before any\n   action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the five\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with masonry\n   work, or overriding a site safety officer's or foreman's judgment,\n   is a second HARD, PERMANENT block (`:scope-excluded-action`),\n   evaluated unconditionally on content -- defense-in-depth on top of\n   the allowlist check: even if a future change somehow widened the\n   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   masonry-work-execution/safety-override language wherever it\n   appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a structural-integrity/\n  falling-material/wall-collapse/scaffold-hazard/crew-fatigue\n  observation -- it never resolves the concern itself, and the\n  robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard data,\" never \"here is\n  what I recommend doing about site safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  masonry-materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  masonry-work-execution or safety-officer-override attempt, which is\n  why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate vs.\n  structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"masonry\", \"wall\", \"safety\", \"foreman\") can accidentally match\ninside the mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly\nthe risk this vertical's own vocabulary (a job-site coordination actor\nworking adjacent to masonry work necessarily discusses those concepts\nto do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased carelessly.\n`bricklaying.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively\nas finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed with the masonry\nwork\", \"authorize the wall construction\", \"override the foreman's\nsafety judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example phrasing),\nnever bare nouns, and\n`bricklaying.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for worker ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for masonry\ntask ...\", \"... routed for site safety officer review\") WITHOUT\ncontaining any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-\nphrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this\nsession -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development.\n`bricklaying.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercises the same invariant through the full graph run,\nconfirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`\nregardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7112\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7112` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7112`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 21 tests, 45 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-wall-construction` or\n  `:approve-mortar-joint` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate list (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for\n  `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`): rejected outright per the task's\n  explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means\n  the *capability* to finalize a masonry-work-execution decision\n  exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For\n  a decision this severe (falling materials, wall collapse, scaffold\n  hazards), the capability itself must not exist. The only op related\n  to site-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces\n  the need for human/site-safety-officer attention and never itself\n  decides, authorizes, or defers a masonry-work decision for later\n  automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold masonry-materials\n  order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft escalate\n  gate, not a hard block; only the masonry-work-execution/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"masonry\" \"wall\"\n  \"safety\" \"foreman\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known\n  self-trip bug class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked\n  example for this exact task), and per the explicit instruction to\n  phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS,\n  never bare nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  masonry/wall/safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging\n  job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own\n  legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale\n  text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Merging `worker` and `site` into a single entity (only checking one\n  provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered worker AND independently-registered site)\n  matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/site record must be\n  independently verified/registered before any action\" and the\n  established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with\n  two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered worker AND\n   site (`:no-worker`/`:no-site`). Not a masonry-work-execution or\n   safety-officer decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry a masonry-work-execution decision (structurally\n   absent from the op's field shape; any such attempt via free text is\n   caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not\n   authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about a specific\n   wall-laying or mortar-joint step -- it is a calendar/logistics\n   action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a structural-integrity/\n   falling-material/wall-collapse/scaffold-hazard/crew-fatigue concern\n   to a human/site safety officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally,\n   at every confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the\n   ONLY channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced,\n   and it explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything\n   itself -- the site safety officer or foreman reviews and acts\n   entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- masonry-materials procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a masonry-work-execution or\n   authorization decision of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics\n   action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000\n   always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na masonry-work-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a specific\nwall-laying or mortar-joint step) or a site-safety-officer/foreman-\njudgment override -- these categories are structurally absent, not\ngated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`bricklaying.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) five concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`bricklaying.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`bricklaying.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-masonry-work-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-wall-construction`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-foreman-judgment`), and (c) a\ncontent-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op` field\n(`bricklaying.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with masonry work or override a foreman's\nsafety judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`bricklaying.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-foreman`).\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 21, :assertions 45, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Bricklaying Site Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :worker-verification :site-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7112"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7112"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7112)), (:test/simulator :bricklaying.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7112 — Bricklayers and Related Workers Job-Site Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7112)))